WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:06.082
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level five.

2
00:00:06.382 --> 00:00:09.814
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:10.114 --> 00:00:18.874
Unit seventeen: Academic Conference Papers. Today you will talk about plans and arrangements with will be doing, be due to and be about to.

4
00:00:19.174 --> 00:00:21.884
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:22.833 --> 00:00:26.078
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:26.917 --> 00:00:28.429
Conference proposal.

7
00:00:29.417 --> 00:00:34.040
Conference proposal means a document that offers a paper or talk for a conference.

8
00:00:34.650 --> 00:00:37.346
The conference proposal was accepted in March.

9
00:00:37.950 --> 00:00:41.299
Your turn. Say: Conference proposal.

10
00:00:44.133 --> 00:00:45.866
Conference proposal.

11
00:00:46.617 --> 00:00:48.082
Abstract submission.

12
00:00:49.083 --> 00:00:53.254
Abstract submission means sending a short summary of a study for approval.

13
00:00:53.850 --> 00:00:56.546
Abstract submission closes on Friday.

14
00:00:57.150 --> 00:01:00.371
Your turn. Say: Abstract submission.

15
00:01:03.233 --> 00:01:04.768
Abstract submission.

16
00:01:05.517 --> 00:01:06.611
Peer feedback.

17
00:01:07.617 --> 00:01:10.998
Peer feedback means comments from other researchers on your work.

18
00:01:11.583 --> 00:01:13.594
Peer feedback improved the argument.

19
00:01:14.183 --> 00:01:16.778
Your turn. Say: Peer feedback.

20
00:01:19.633 --> 00:01:20.739
Peer feedback.

21
00:01:21.483 --> 00:01:23.065
Presentation slide.

22
00:01:24.050 --> 00:01:28.581
Presentation slide means a single page shown to an audience during a talk.

23
00:01:29.183 --> 00:01:31.996
Each presentation slide carries one idea.

24
00:01:32.583 --> 00:01:35.990
Your turn. Say: Presentation slide.

25
00:01:38.833 --> 00:01:40.554
Presentation slide.

26
00:01:41.317 --> 00:01:42.538
Q&A session.

27
00:01:43.550 --> 00:01:47.465
Q&A session means a time when the audience asks the speaker questions.

28
00:01:48.050 --> 00:01:50.595
The Q and A session lasted twenty minutes.

29
00:01:51.183 --> 00:01:54.347
Your turn. Say: Q&A session.

30
00:01:57.200 --> 00:01:58.422
Q&A session.

31
00:01:59.183 --> 00:02:00.672
Academic networking.

32
00:02:01.683 --> 00:02:06.957
Academic networking means meeting other scholars to share ideas and make contacts.

33
00:02:07.550 --> 00:02:10.281
Academic networking led to a new project.

34
00:02:10.883 --> 00:02:14.058
Your turn. Say: Academic networking.

35
00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:18.702
Academic networking.

36
00:02:19.450 --> 00:02:20.776
Conference panel.

37
00:02:21.783 --> 00:02:26.302
Conference panel means a group of experts who discuss a topic together.

38
00:02:26.917 --> 00:02:29.624
The conference panel debated open access.

39
00:02:30.217 --> 00:02:33.473
Your turn. Say: Conference panel.

40
00:02:36.333 --> 00:02:37.671
Conference panel.

41
00:02:38.417 --> 00:02:40.033
Research dissemination.

42
00:02:41.017 --> 00:02:45.222
Research dissemination means sharing research results widely with others.

43
00:02:45.817 --> 00:02:49.186
Research dissemination includes talks and articles.

44
00:02:49.783 --> 00:02:52.888
Your turn. Say: Research dissemination.

45
00:02:55.733 --> 00:02:57.605
Research dissemination.

46
00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:01.911
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:03:02.750 --> 00:03:10.371
Visal, a programme says 'Dr Lee will be presenting at ten, and the panel is due to start at noon.' Why different forms?

48
00:03:11.483 --> 00:03:24.892
They are future forms. Will be + -ing means action in progress at that time. Be due to means scheduled by an official plan. Be about to means very soon: The session is about to begin.

49
00:03:25.983 --> 00:03:27.542
What about arrangements?

50
00:03:28.650 --> 00:03:40.497
Use the present continuous for a fixed arrangement: I am presenting on Friday. Be to is formal and for official plans: The delegates are to register by nine.

51
00:03:41.583 --> 00:03:43.293
Why use them in academic writing?

52
00:03:44.383 --> 00:03:56.617
They make timing clear. Will be + -ing sounds polite and avoids saying you decided it: We will be discussing the results. Be about to sounds immediate, so avoid it for distant events.

53
00:03:57.767 --> 00:03:59.592
Part three: grammar.

54
00:04:00.450 --> 00:04:06.142
Grammar focus: Future plans: will be plus -ing and be due to and be about to.

55
00:04:06.442 --> 00:04:07.570
Look at the pattern.

56
00:04:08.283 --> 00:04:10.794
Dr Lee will be presenting at ten tomorrow.

57
00:04:11.683 --> 00:04:13.648
The panel is due to start at noon.

58
00:04:14.550 --> 00:04:16.259
The session is about to begin.

59
00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:27.125
Will be + -ing = in progress at a future time. Be due to = official schedule. Be about to = very soon. Present continuous = fixed arrangement.

60
00:04:27.883 --> 00:04:31.708
Be careful! Don't say: The talk is about start now.

61
00:04:33.667 --> 00:04:36.038
Say: The talk is about to start now.

62
00:04:36.338 --> 00:04:38.337
Say be about to + base verb.

63
00:04:39.133 --> 00:04:41.716
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:42.500 --> 00:04:43.780
Before the Conference.

65
00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:48.738
A conference proposal must be accepted before the abstract submission can begin.

66
00:04:49.733 --> 00:04:53.834
By next week, many researchers will be preparing their presentation slides.

67
00:04:54.833 --> 00:04:59.979
The conference panel is due to start at noon, and the Q&A session is about to follow.

68
00:05:00.967 --> 00:05:05.927
Peer feedback received at the event will often be used to improve the final paper.

69
00:05:06.933 --> 00:05:12.602
Academic networking and research dissemination are why scholars will be travelling from many countries.

70
00:05:13.700 --> 00:05:18.015
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:18.850 --> 00:05:21.372
This time tomorrow, I blank my paper.

72
00:05:21.672 --> 00:05:25.546
A, will be presenting. B, present. C, am presented.

73
00:05:29.100 --> 00:05:31.181
The answer is A: will be presenting.

74
00:05:32.317 --> 00:05:36.151
A time when the audience asks the speaker questions is a blank session.

75
00:05:36.451 --> 00:05:40.000
A, Q&A. B, panel. C, slide.

76
00:05:43.533 --> 00:05:46.090
The answer is A: Q&A.

77
00:05:47.250 --> 00:05:48.924
Which sentence is correct?

78
00:05:49.224 --> 00:05:54.898
A, The talk is about start.. B, The talk is about to start.. C, The talk about is to start..

79
00:05:58.433 --> 00:06:01.280
The answer is B: The talk is about to start..

80
00:06:02.417 --> 00:06:05.403
Sharing research results widely is research blank.

81
00:06:05.703 --> 00:06:09.415
A, proposal. B, networking. C, dissemination.

82
00:06:12.967 --> 00:06:15.164
The answer is C: dissemination.

83
00:06:16.317 --> 00:06:20.545
According to the reading, why will scholars travel from many countries?

84
00:06:20.845 --> 00:06:26.205
A, networking and dissemination. B, slide design. C, ticket prices.

85
00:06:29.767 --> 00:06:32.567
The answer is A: networking and dissemination.

86
00:06:33.767 --> 00:06:37.162
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:38.017 --> 00:06:43.580
Why is presenting research at an academic conference valuable, beyond just publishing a paper?

88
00:06:52.383 --> 00:06:53.849
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:54.149 --> 00:07:21.138
Presenting at a conference is valuable because it allows direct conversation. In a Q&A session, experts will be asking questions that may reveal weaknesses or new ideas, and peer feedback can improve the paper before publication. It also builds academic networking, which may lead to cooperation, and it spreads the research to people who might not read the journal.

90
00:07:21.438 --> 00:07:23.206
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:24.117 --> 00:07:30.705
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and future plans: will be plus -ing and be due to and be about to.

92
00:07:31.005 --> 00:07:36.072
Today's value is public engagement. Share knowledge so that everyone can learn from it.

93
00:07:36.372 --> 00:07:38.827
See you in Unit eighteen. Goodbye!

94
00:07:39.127 --> 00:07:42.093
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

